configuration.analysisConfiguration:
{
  "revapi": {
    "semver": {
      "ignore": {
        "enabled": true,
        "versionIncreaseAllows": {
          "major": "breaking",
          "minor": "nonBreaking",
          "patch": "equivalent"
        }
      }
    },
    "differences": {
      "ignore": true,
      "differences": [
        {
          "regex": true,
          "code": "java\\.missing\\.(new|old)Class",
          "justification": "A TYPE THAT IS ABSENT FROM BOTH ARCHIVES, NOT A DIFFERENCE BETWEEN THEM, and the narrower half of this problem was fixed at its cause rather than listed here. Two kinds of missing class showed up on the first gated build. The first was jakarta.servlet.HttpServletRequest and friends in odata-filter: resolvable, just not resolved, because they arrive transitively at `provided` scope - that is fixed above with resolveTransitiveProvidedDependencies, and it is fixed there and not here on purpose, because ignoring a class revapi COULD have loaded turns an incorrect analysis into a quiet one. The second kind cannot be fixed that way: spring-security-config's HttpSecurity declares saml2Login(), ldapAuthentication() and oauth2Client(), whose types live in spring-security-saml2-service-provider, spring-security-ldap and spring-security-oauth2-client - jars this platform does not depend on and should not start depending on to satisfy a checker. Twenty-three of them, reachable only through Spring's own types, which the design-advisory exemption below already covers. They are absent from the old archive and the new one alike, so they can never constitute a difference between two releases; what they would do armed is fail every build forever. If a type of OURS ever goes missing that is java.class.removed, which stays armed."
        },
        {
          "regex": true,
          "code": "java\\.class\\.(externalClassExposedInAPI|nonPublicPartOfAPI)",
          "justification": "THE ONLY BLANKET EXEMPTION IN THIS FILE, AND IT IS NOT A COMPATIBILITY DIFFERENCE. These two codes are the whole of revapi's API-DESIGN advisory family, and both are computed from the NEW archive ALONE: they fire identically on a build with no source change at all, and they would fire on the very first release, when there is nothing to be compatible with. Nothing they report is a statement about how this release differs from the last one, which is the only question this gate exists to ask. Concretely, externalClassExposedInAPI named several hundred Spring types - ApplicationContext, ResolvableType, TypeDescriptor, Environment - all reached transitively from one @Bean method returning a LocalValidatorFactoryBean, and nonPublicPartOfAPI named package-private WireMock and Testcontainers internals reached from test-support's fixture accessors. Twenty-five Spring Boot starters whose purpose is to hand Spring types to a Spring application, and a test-support jar whose purpose is to hand out configured containers, cannot answer either; the only 'fix' would be to stop exposing the libraries they exist to expose. Left armed, they failed the fourth and the twenty-second module of the first gated build for reasons unrelated to compatibility, which is exactly how a gate gets switched off and never switched back on. Every code that IS a compatibility statement - java.method.removed, java.method.parameterTypeChanged, java.method.addedToInterface, java.class.kindChanged, java.field.removed, java.class.removed and the rest - stays fully armed and is governed by the semver rule above."
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
